Data-free Multistep Diffusion Distillation Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2610.19305v1 [cs.CV] 13 Oct 2026 Data-free Multistep Diffusion Distillation Ngoc Duy Tran Affiliation: Deakin Applied Artificial Intelligence Initiative, Deakin University, Australia Email: s22414149@deakin.edu.au Kien Do Duc Thanh Nguyen Affiliation: VinSmart Future, Vietnam School of Information Technology, Deakin University, Australia Truyen Tran Affiliation: Deakin Applied Artificial Intelligence Initiative, Deakin University, Australia Abstract One-step diffusion distillation achieves strong performance on small datasets but remains insufficient for high-resolution or complex text-to-image settings. Extending to multistep distillation often requires training data, suffers from early-step degradation, inconsistent outputs across sampling budgets, and incurs high computational cost. We identify that these limitations stem from simultaneous training, where a single adapted network approximates multiple score functions of different student distributions at once, causing biased distribution matching and unstable optimization. Therefore, we propose Progressive Multistep Data-free Distillation (PMDD), which assigns a dedicated adapted network per student distribution while avoiding the memory overhead of maintaining multiple adapted networks. Combined with a stage-wise unforget loss to preserve earlier-step generations, PMDD reduces optimization bias in the student, provides faster convergence, and a single unified model that supports multiple inference budgets. Extensive experiments show that PMDD matches or outperforms data-free few-step baselines at every multistep budget, reaches FID 1.99 on ImageNet 64 × 	imes 64 in two steps, below its 512-NFE teacher (2.32), and achieves FID 7.76 on zero-shot COCO 512 × 	imes 512, while requiring 4–5 × 	imes less training compute than data-free DMDv2 to support three inference budgets. Figure 1 : Generations of PMDD and data-free DMDv2 across different inference budgets (NFE: neural function evaluations). DMDv2 shows strong inconsistency across inference budgets and between previously distilled 1-step and 2-step models. In contrast, PMDD maintains consistent structure and high-quality outputs across all sampling budgets using a single model. 1 Introduction Diffusion models have attained significant success in generative tasks, demonstrating state-of-the-art performance in a wide range of domains such as image generation [ 41 , 42 , 12 , 39 ] , audio synthesis [ 5 , 15 ] , and text generation [ 1 , 10 , 24 ] . Diffusion-based image generation models adopt an iterative denoising process which gradually removes noise from a noisy intermediate sample to reconstruct a high-quality image. However, the sampling process is inherently slow, typically requiring hundreds or thousands of neural function evaluations (NFEs), which makes the models expensive for many real-world applications. To overcome this limitation, recent research has explored distilling a (diffusion) teacher into a (diffusion) student. These approaches fall into two families. ​ T ​ r ​ a ​ j ​ e ​ c ​ t ​ o ​ r ​ y − m ​ a ​ t ​ c ​ h ​ i ​ n ​ g \emph{Trajectory-matching} methods [ 25 , 37 , 40 , 26 , 6 , 14 ] regress the student onto the teacher’s deterministic ODE trajectory, and typically require real samples or adversarial training to match their teachers. ​ D ​ i ​ s ​ t ​ r ​ i ​ b ​ u ​ t ​ i ​ o ​ n − m ​ a ​ t ​ c ​ h ​ i ​ n ​ g \emph{Distribution-matching} methods, motivated by frameworks such as GMMNs [ 18 ] and GANs [ 8 ] , instead learn a one-step mapping x 0 | ϕ , T = g ϕ ​ ( x T , T ) x_{0|\phi,T}=g_{\phi}\left(x_{T},Tight) from Gaussian noise x T ∼ 𝒩 ⁡ ( 0 , ℐ ) x_{T}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(0,\mathcal{I}ight) to clean data, and align the ​ d ​ i ​ s ​ t ​ r ​ i ​ b ​ u ​ t ​ i ​ o ​ n \emph{distribution} of student outputs with that of the teacher. An adapted network φ \varphi is introduced to estimate the score function of the student distribution and guide it toward the teacher distribution. While effective on small- and medium-scale datasets such as CIFAR-10 and ImageNet 64 × 64 64	imes 64 , one-step distillation struggles with complex text-image compositions and high-resolution synthesis [ 27 , 31 , 51 , 50 , 55 ] . Therefore, extending distillation to few steps is necessary to achieve higher fidelity generation while preserving most of the sampling speedup. To enable multistep sampling, prior approaches naively extend the one-step framework, allocating a single adapted network φ \varphi to simultaneously approximate N N score functions corresponding to distributions produced by g ϕ ​ ( x t i , t i ) g_{\phi}\left(x_{t_{i}},t_{i}ight) ( i = 1 , … , N i=1,...,N ) [ 53 , 50 , 38 ] . This single design choice is, we argue, the root cause of the field’s current limitations. Forcing one network to fit N N heterogeneous score targets within a shared parameter space induces ​ n ​ e ​ g ​ a ​ t ​ i ​ v ​ e ​ t ​ r ​ a ​ n ​ s ​ f ​ e ​ r \emph{negativetransfer} : the network cannot fit any single target well, yielding biased distribution matching at every timestep. Compounding this, the student’s intermediate predictions are imperfect and continuously evolving during training, the student thus optimizes against drifting, high-variance targets. Together these effects produce distribution mismatch, amplify error accumulation, and destabilize optimization. These theoretical pathologies have concrete consequences. First, increasing the number of sampling steps often yields only marginal improvement, or even degradation [ 50 , 53 ] , forcing existing methods to heavily rely on ​ 𝐫 ​ 𝐞 ​ 𝐚 ​ 𝐥 − 𝐢 ​ 𝐦 ​ 𝐚 ​ 𝐠 ​ 𝐞  oldsymbol{\emph{real-image}} adversarial training to boost visual quality. Second, training is extremely costly: MMD runs each experiment on 256 TPU v5e chips [ 38 ] , while DMDv2 consumes 64 A100 GPUs per model [ 50 ] . Third, the simultaneous objective lacks consistency across sampling budgets: a model trained for N N -step inference produces high-quality output only at N N steps, while 1 1 to N − 1 N{-}1 steps degrade. Supporting N N budgets therefore requires training N N separate models, multiplying substantial training cost. We introduce Progressive Multistep Data-free Distillation (PMDD) , a novel framework whose central mechanism directly removes the single-shared-network bottleneck. PMDD assigns a ​ d ​ e ​ d ​ i ​ c ​ a ​ t ​ e ​ d \emph{dedicated} adapted network to each student score function g ϕ ​ ( x t i , t i ) g_{\phi}\left(x_{t_{i}},t_{i}ight) , so every stage is modeled accurately and free of negative transfer. To avoid the linear memory growth this would naively imply, PMDD adopts a ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ g ​ r ​ e ​ s ​ s ​ i ​ v ​ e \emph{progressive} schedule that incrementally transforms a K K -step student into a K + 1 K{+}1 -step student, building from 1 1 up to N N steps. Each new stage is trained on samples drawn from the ​ p ​ r ​ e ​ v ​ i ​ o ​ u ​ s ​ l ​ y ​ d ​ i ​ s ​ t ​ i ​ l ​ l ​ e ​ d \emph{previouslydistilled} student, which already approximates the relevant marginal well, replacing the drifting, high-variance targets of simultaneous training with stable ones, and sharply accelerating convergence. Finally, an ​ u ​ n ​ f ​ o ​ r ​ g ​ e ​ t ​ t ​ i ​ n ​ g ​ l ​ o ​ s ​ s \emph{unforgettingloss} regularizes against the previous student, preserving earlier-step behavior so that one PMDD model produces consistent, high-quality output across ​ a ​ l ​ l \emph{all} inference budgets, a property no prior data-free method offers. We evaluate our approach across various tasks, including conditional image generation on CIFAR-10 [ 16 ] , ImageNet 64 × 	imes 64 [ 36 ] , and zero-shot text-to-image generation on MS COCO 512 × 	imes 512 [ 19 ] . PMDD improves with the number of steps and matches or outperforms data-free few-step baselines at every multistep budget, including FS-SiD [ 53 ] , reaching FID 7.76 on MS-COCO 2014-30k, while supporting three inference budgets at 4–5 × 	imes lower training compute than data-free DMDv2. We further show that a single PMDD model maintains strong consistency across inference budgets, with higher step counts progressively refining detail while preserving global structure, and that the framework generalizes to higher-resolution 1024 × 1024 1024	imes 1024 setting. 2 Preliminary 2.1 Data-free One-step Diffusion Distillation Data-free one-step diffusion distillation aims to train a generator g ϕ ​ ( x T , T ) g_{\phi}\left(x_{T},Tight) (the student ) that directly maps Gaussian noise x T ∼ 𝒩 ⁡ ( 0 , I ) x_{T}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(0,\mathrm{I}ight) to data samples x 0 x_{0} under the guidance of a pretrained diffusion model (the teacher ). A common strategy is to align the student distribution p ϕ ​ ( x 0 ) p_{\phi}\left(x_{0}ight) with the teacher distribution p θ ​ ( x 0 ) p_{	heta}\left(x_{0}ight) . This is typically achieved by minimizing the reverse KL divergence: ℒ KL ( ϕ ) := D KL ( p ϕ ( x 0 ) ∥ p θ ( x 0 ) ) , \mathcal{L}_{	ext{KL}}\left(\phiight):=D_{	ext{KL}}\left(p_{\phi}\left(x_{0}ight)\|p_{	heta}\left(x_{0}ight)ight), (1) which differs from the forward KL divergence D KL ( p 𝒟 ( x 0 ) ∥ p ϕ ( x 0 ) ) D_{	ext{KL}}\left(p_{\mathcal{D}}\left(x_{0}ight)\|p_{\phi}\left(x_{0}ight)ight) in data-dependent distillation. In practice, this objective is optimized through its variational upper bound [ 12 , 42 ] , which reduces to the denoised-sample matching (DM) loss: ℒ ⁡ ( ϕ ) := 𝔼 x T , x 0 | ϕ , T , t , ϵ , x t ​ [ w x ​ ( t ) ​ ‖ x ϕ ​ ( x t , t ) − x θ ​ ( x t , t ) ‖ 2 2 ] \mathcal{L}\left(\phiight):=\mathbb{E}_{x_{T},x_{0|\phi,T},t,\epsilon,x_{t}}\left[w_{x}\left(tight)\left\|x_{\phi}\left(x_{t},tight)-x_{	heta}\left(x_{t},t