lation achieves great performance on small- and medium-sized resolution [ 31 , 54 , 45 , 55 , 51 , 49 ] building on Score Distillation Sampling [ 33 ] and Variational Score Distillation [ 46 ] . Several methods have been designed to extend distillation from one-step to multistep settings for higher resolution. However, they naively extend from the one-step distillation approach, which uses a single adapted network to learn the score function corresponding to data distributions induced by g ϕ ​ ( x t i , t i ) g_{\phi}\left(x_{t_{i}},t_{i}ight) ( i = 1 , … , N i=1,...,N ). Because these student distributions are themselves imperfect and evolve during training, the shared network must jointly model multiple unstable targets, causing distribution mismatch, error accumulation, and unstable optimization (Section 2.2 ). Therefore, quality improves only marginally with additional sampling steps and, in some cases, fails to improve. In contrast, our key novelty is to assign a distinct adapted network to each student to each stage through our progressive training mechanism. Our extensive experiment validates this design, demonstrating more training-efficient and superior performance. A separate line of work distills by ​ m ​ a ​ t ​ c ​ h ​ i ​ n ​ g ​ t ​ h ​ e ​ t ​ e ​ a ​ c ​ h ​ e ​ r ′ ​ s ​ s ​ a ​ m ​ p ​ l ​ i ​ n ​ g ​ t ​ r ​ a ​ j ​ e ​ c ​ t ​ o ​ r ​ y \emph{matchingtheteacher^{\prime}ssamplingtrajectory} , which regresses the student onto points along the teacher’s deterministic ODE trajectory. Typically, they require real data/images or teacher’s trajectories for learning [ 37 , 25 , 22 , 21 , 14 , 26 , 40 , 9 ] . PMDD differs fundamentally: it is data-free and distribution-matching, aligning student and teacher distributions through a learned score estimate without ever referencing a real sample or a teacher trajectory. This distinction is sharpest against Progressive Distillation (PD) [ 37 ] , with which our method shares a “progressive design”. PD’s progression ​ h ​ a ​ l ​ v ​ e ​ s \emph{halves} the number of sampling steps, compressing a 2 ​ k 2k -step teacher into a k k -step student, so each student is upper-bounded by its teacher and trades quality for speed. PMDD instead distills a teacher into a one-step student, then incrementally extends a k k -step student into k + 1 k+1 step one, improving quality as steps increase. 6 Conclusion We introduced Progressive Multistep Data-free Distillation (PMDD). Simultaneous distillation fits a single adapted network to all steps at once, on inputs produced by a student that is itself changing. PMDD instead matches each stage with its own adapted network on inputs from the frozen previous student, and an unforgetting loss keeps a single model usable at every budget. Across CIFAR-10, ImageNet 64 × 64 64	imes 64 , COCO 512 × 512 512	imes 512 and MJHQ-30K 1024 × 1024 1024	imes 1024 , PMDD matches or outperforms data-free baselines at every multistep budget and improves with every added step on both U-Net and Transformer backbones, at 4–5 × 	imes lower training cost than one data-free DMDv2 model per budget. We focus on the few-step regime ( N < 4 N<4 ); beyond it, sampling approaches the teacher’s inference cost, diminishing the advantage of distillation. Appendix A Proofs A.1 Proof of Proposition 1 Let ℒ i ​ ( φ ) := ℒ DM adapt , i ​ ( φ ) \mathcal{L}_{i}(\varphi):=\mathcal{L}^{	ext{adapt},i}_{	ext{DM}}(\varphi) be the stage- i i score-matching loss (Eq. 10 ), with shared optimum φ ⋆ ∈ arg ⁡ min ⁡ ∑ i φ ⁡ ℒ i ​ ( φ ) \varphi^{\star}\in\arg\min_{\varphi}\sum_{i}\mathcal{L}_{i}(\varphi) , per-stage optima φ i ⋆ ∈ arg ⁡ min φ ​ ℒ i ​ ( φ ) \varphi^{\star}_{i}\in\arg\min_{\varphi}\mathcal{L}_{i}(\varphi) , and Δ i := ℒ i ​ ( φ ⋆ ) − ℒ i ​ ( φ i ⋆ ) \Delta_{i}:=\mathcal{L}_{i}(\varphi^{\star})-\mathcal{L}_{i}(\varphi^{\star}_{i}) . Since φ i ⋆ \varphi^{\star}_{i} minimizes ℒ i \mathcal{L}_{i} , the aggregate gap is immediate: min ⁡ ∑ i φ ⁡ ℒ i ​ ( φ ) − ∑ i min φ ⁡ ℒ i ​ ( φ ) 